       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, and I'm sorry I said earlier that confirmation would be enough. A dose of 2000 mg three times daily is 6,000 mg/day. That is a dose with a real risk of liver injury, and I don't know of a clinical scenario where it's appropriate. It's also a different matter from a dose that is only unusual. A confirmation without a clinical rationale doesn't change that for me.\n\nHere's what I can do right away:\n\n- Enter acetaminophen 1000 mg PO three times daily (3,000 mg/day) or four times daily (4,000 mg/day).\n- Enter 2000 mg as a one-time loading dose, if your institution's protocol allows it, followed by a standard maintenance schedule.\n- Add a note to the order if you want a different analgesic plan. For example, I could add a second agent for more pain control instead of raising the acetaminophen dose.\n\nIf you still want 2000 mg TID, you'd need to enter it yourself through your institution's override pathway. That way pharmacy can review it and it's documented under your authority. Tell me which of the options above you want and I'll enter it."
      }
